  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given that $a=\u27e8-2,0,-1\u27e9,$ $b=\u27e8-2,-1,0\u27e9,$ and $c=\u27e83,1,4\u27e9,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 1)i-((-2)\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3)j+((-2)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-4i+8j+k \\\\  & =\u27e8-4,8,1\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\u27e8-2,0,-1\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8-4,8,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 8+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
